 "best": "wavlm_both",
 "headline": "Can a detector catch voice generators it has never seen?",
 "subhead": "On new generators it caught 78% of fakes, up from 43%, and wrongly flagged 7% of real clips, down from 34%. But it caught only 2 of 3 clones of one Indian-accented voice.",
 "method_html": "\n<ol>\n<li><b>Data.</b> 5,760 six-second clips. Fakes: recordings of 54 public figures collected from the internet by researchers, 69 named speech generators (MLAAD), 11 vocoder sets (WaveFake) and 13 attacks (ASVspoof 2021). Real: the same public figures, audiobooks, crowd recordings (FLEURS), meeting headsets (AMI), Indian-accented English (Common Voice) and one studio voice (LJSpeech). We made no fake audio.</li>\n<li><b>Split by whole generator.</b> About 20% of the generators in each dataset were held out for the test, 10% to choose settings, and the rest to train. Real clips were split by speaker or book. The AMI meeting set was held out entirely.</li>\n<li><b>Detector.</b> WavLM listens; a small classifier on its fifth layer learns real against fake, trained on clean and phone-quality copies. Eight layers, three regularisation strengths and seven training lengths were compared on the check split only. The test split was used once.</li>\n<li><b>Phone line.</b> 300 to 3,400 Hz, 8 kHz, standard phone compression.</li>\n<li><b>First test.</b> The charts further down are the first version, tested on 12 held-out people from the first dataset only.</li>\n</ol>",
 "caveats_html": "\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fakes in an Indian accent.</b> Three clones of one Indian-accented voice: 2 of 3 caught on clean audio, 1 of 3 over a simulated phone line. The first version caught 3 of 3 on clean audio. No fake Indian-accent voices were in training, so the detector may treat the accent as a sign of a real voice. Three clips cannot show this.</li>\n<li><b>Small tests.</b> 223 fake clips from 19 held-out generators, 10 to 20 clips each. Indian-accent real speech: 46 clips.</li>\n<li><b>Still missed.</b> Two Japanese vocoder sets, and some modern generators (Llasa-8B, Cartesia Sonic-3, Kyutai, minimax, ASVspoof attacks A12 and A16), are caught on under 70% of clips.</li>\n<li><b>More false alarms on the first dataset.</b> On the first 12 people, real clips wrongly flagged rose from 1% to 12%.</li>\n<li><b>Recording differences.</b> Real and fake clips mostly come from different sources, so part of any score may reflect the recording, not the voice.</li>\n<li><b>Real calls are mostly genuine.</b> If 1 call in 1,000 is fake, a 7% false-alarm rate wrongly flags about 70 genuine callers for each fake call.</li>\n<li><b>Languages.</b> English only. Other languages and real phone calls are untested.</li>\n<li><b>A signal, not proof.</b> Use it with other checks, such as a call-back.</li>\n</ul>",
